    "title": "How I Built My Own GPT Assistant",
    "teaser": "A liquid-glass chat interface powered by NVIDIA Nemotron, with theme-switching and search mode live, image gen and OAuth still in progress.",
    "tags": [
      "GPT-4o",
      "AI",
      "Build"
    ],
    "paragraphs": [
      "PrashantGPT isn't a wrapper around a single API call — it's a full chat interface I built from scratch, running on NVIDIA's Nemotron models on the backend instead of the usual OpenAI/Anthropic API. The frontend is a liquid-glass interface with four full themes (Aurora, Sunset, Arctic, Obsidian), each one swapping the entire color system, gradients, and glow effects live via a draggable floating theme switcher — no page reload, no flicker.",
      "The part I'm most proud of isn't the chat itself, it's the \"Prompt Inspiration\" generator built into the input bar. Instead of staring at a blank textbox, you pick topics — Blockchain, Agentic AI, Web Design, Banking & Finance, Dehradun, whatever fits — and it calls a dedicated backend endpoint to draft a tailored starter prompt for you. It's a small feature, but it changes how often I actually use the tool versus just thinking about using it.",
      "There's a toggleable web search mode already working, baked directly into the same input — flip it on and the same textbox routes to a search API instead of the chat model, rendering live results inline instead of a chat reply. Image generation and Google OAuth login are both wired into the interface too, though they're still under construction on the backend — the UI is ready, the plumbing behind them isn't fully live yet.",
      "Every design decision here came from using it daily: the chat history sidebar, the auto-resizing input, the streaming-style typing indicator — all added because I got annoyed at their absence, not because a spec asked for them. That's also why image gen and login are shipping as visible-but-unfinished rather than hidden — I'd rather build in the open and finish them properly than fake a demo.",
      "It now lives at gpt.prashantbhatt.net and already handles a good chunk of my day-to-day drafting, research, and quick lookups — proof that a genuinely useful personal AI tool doesn't need a team behind it, just enough friction in the free tools to justify building your own."
    ]
